name=jira-ticket
version=1.2.0
description=Create and manage Jira tickets (bugs/tasks) via acli, linking to the configured epic and active sprint. Use when: creating a Jira ticket, raising a bug, logging a task, or moving an issue to a sprint.
argument_hint=Optional: the issue type and summary, e.g. "Bug login button does nothing"
requires=
assets=jira-ticket
next_step=acli jira auth|Authenticate the Atlassian CLI. Transitions and assignment go through `acli`, so an unauthenticated CLI leaves a ticket created but unmoved and unassigned.|`acli jira auth status` fails
next_step=export JIRA_EMAIL="you@company.com"|Creating and re-describing issues goes through the Jira REST API, which authenticates as you: the account email comes from $JIRA_EMAIL and the API token from the OS keychain, never from the committed config. Put the export in your shell profile — it is per-user, not per-repo — and store the token once using the recipe in the header of jira-sprint.sh. Without it every script fails before it calls Jira. If it is unset, check `acli --version` too — an unset email usually means this machine has had no Jira setup at all.|`printenv JIRA_EMAIL` prints nothing
next_step=Fill in .agents/jira-ticket-context.md|The installer seeds this as a placeholder reading "No custom instructions yet." It is where this project records what it does differently from the shared templates — its own acceptance criteria, required fields or labels, and board wording. The skill reads it before writing every ticket, so until it says something the shared defaults are used unchanged.|.agents/jira-ticket-context.md is missing, or still reads "No custom instructions yet."
next_step=Set jira_board_id and jira_epic_key|Without a board id the skill cannot find the active sprint, and without an epic key new tickets are created unparented. Both are blank by default because no cross-repo value is sensible — the skill skips those steps rather than guessing.|`skills.sh config show` reports jira_board_id or jira_epic_key as SOURCE=default
next_step=Install Node 18 or newer|Unlike the installer, this skill's payload is Node: descriptions are converted to ADF before being sent, because Jira renders a plain-text description as literal markdown. Node is the whole requirement — the converter ships in the skill's scripts, has no dependencies and installs nothing, so it needs neither npm nor network access.|`node --version` fails or reports below 18
